<% record_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/blob/main/docs/design_record.md" %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  Complexity rises at emergence and then stops rising on every substrate the programme has
  tested from a random start, and the
  <%= link_to "host–parasite sweep", finding_path("complexity-under-contest") %> found one
  priced arm that kept rising on a single measured run. A run that starts from random tapes
  spends most of its budget waiting for replicators, and a treatment can only work on what is
  left. DESIGN §1.3 sweep 11 starts from replicators instead. <strong>Once a treatment is
  switched on in a world that has already made them, does the existing replicator keep
  getting more complicated, and does it hold at all?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Each parent is an economy-off control of sweep 9 whose last stored world is at least half
  replicators by the orientation-aware census. Its terminal world is continued for another
  20 000 epochs under four treatments, seeds 1001–1003 each, paired by (parent, seed): the
  continuation under the parent's own dynamics, an economy with influx 2048, a rich economy
  with influx 8192 (both with steal 1024, a stock cap of 32 768 and loss 0.5), and host mode.
</p>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong>
  (<%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>, 2026-09-25, "Runs that start from an
  emerged world", and its six clarifications). Each child held its replicators where the
  last-decile median <span class="mono">replicator_share</span> stayed high and the share
  never sat at the floor, and its complexity rises, plateaus or is mixed on
  <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> over the samples whose dominant tape
  self-replicates. Each treated arm is a one-sided sign test against the continuation over
  paired children, printed but not read where the arm relapses more often than the
  continuation; H-persistence says no continuation child relapses. A second, held-out
  reading (2026-09-25, "The from-emerged economy arms, confirmed on held-out parents") was
  written after the first ten parents had been seen, and reads the same complexity rule on
  the survivors of the parents that qualified afterwards: H4-survivors.
</p>

<% reading = @show.from_emerged %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading&.report&.any? %>
  <p>
    <% if reading.complexity_rise_shown? %>
      <strong>An economy arm reads as keeping complexity rising: see the readings below.</strong>
    <% else %>
      <strong>From an emerged start, neither energy economy can be read as keeping complexity
      rising, and on the held-out survivors the complexity signal does not replicate.</strong>
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.interim? %>
      Not every child of the sweep has finished, so this reading is interim.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <% reading.comparisons.each do |treatment, comparison| %>
      <li>
        <strong><%= treatment.hypothesis %></strong>, <%= treatment.name %>:
        <%= comparison.favouring %> pairs favour the treatment and <%= comparison.against %> the
        continuation<% if comparison.p_value %>, p = <%= metric_value comparison.p_value.to_f %><% end %>,
        <span class="badge <%= comparison.badge_class %>"><%= comparison.reading_label %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
    <li>
      <strong>H-persistence</strong>:
      <span class="badge <%= reading.persistence.badge_class %>"><%= reading.persistence.outcome_label %></span>
      <%= reading.report.persistence_line %>.
    </li>
    <% reading.survivor_tests.each do |test| %>
      <li>
        <strong><%= test.hypothesis %></strong>, held out, <%= test.treatment.name %>:
        <%= test.comparison.favouring %> pairs favour the treatment and
        <%= test.comparison.against %> the continuation, <%= test.comparison.ties %> tie<% if test.comparison.p_value %>,
        p = <%= metric_value test.comparison.p_value.to_f %><% end %>,
        <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.outcome_label %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <% unless reading.complexity_rise_shown? %>
    <p>
      The economy arms relapse more often than the continuation, so the pre-registered rule
      reads neither of them, and the rich economy's lead rests on two parents. The held-out
      reading was written to ask the question again on colonies that survived the treatment, on
      parents nobody had seen: there almost every surviving pair ties, and the seen rich-economy
      signal is not there. So the economy does not keep complexity rising from an emerged start,
      and the question of whether complexity keeps rising stands where the
      <%= link_to "host–parasite finding", finding_path("complexity-under-contest") %> left it.
      What the economy does do, on the same held-out children, is make the dominant replicator
      <%= link_to "copy itself faster", finding_path("copying-gets-faster-under-an-economy") %>.
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <% continuations = reading.persistence.children.size %>
  <% if continuations.positive? %>
    <p>
      The colonies themselves are robust: <%= continuations - reading.persistence.relapsed.size %> of
      <%= continuations %> continuations kept their replicators over 20 000 more epochs.
      <% if reading.persistence.refuted? %>
        Only the strict rule that none relapses is refuted.
      <% end %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored samples at render time, through
    the same readings <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("from-emerged", anchor: "descendant-reading") %>
    draws.
  </p>

  <%= render "experiments/descendant_reading", reading: reading.report %>
  <%= render "experiments/heldout_reading", reading: reading.report %>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No child of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("from-emerged") %> is in this database,
    so there is nothing to read here. This page reads the children at render time and states
    the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Not that an economy cannot raise complexity.</strong> It did not here, on this
    substrate, over 20 000 epochs past an emerged world. A longer budget, a different
    economy or a different world is untested.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Complexity is the instruction count.</strong> The rule reads
    <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> over the samples whose dominant tape
    self-replicates; a replicator that grows more intricate without executing more of its
    bytes reads as flat.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Children are correlated.</strong> Three children share each parent's world, so
    the pairs are not independent colonies, which is why every held test lists the parents
    it rests on.
  </li>
</ul>
